# Pour until a scale says enough has come out, then set the vessel back down.
#
# The controller closes the loop on a weight it reads itself; this goal says how
# much, into what, and of what. Every bound left at 0 falls back to the
# controller's configured value, the same convention Gripper.action uses, so a
# caller with no opinion about tilt rates does not have to invent one.
float64 target_grams

# What the scale reads with the EMPTY receiving vessel on it.
#
# The indicator is read-only -- its RS232 port accepts nothing, so it cannot be
# tared from here -- and the pour is therefore measured as
# (reading - container_grams). This is the only zero the controller has.
#
# It is also a PRECONDITION, not just an offset. Before the first tilt the
# controller waits for a settled reading and refuses the goal if it does not
# match this value, which is what catches a vessel that is not on the scale, the
# wrong vessel, residue left by an earlier pour, and a zero point someone moved
# with the front-panel button. An offset alone catches none of those and pours
# anyway.
float64 container_grams

# What is being poured.
#
# The two classes do not share a flow law and cannot be collapsed into one
# parameter. A liquid's outflow is a continuous, monotone function of tilt, so
# it can be regulated. A granular medium arrives in avalanches whose size is set
# by the aperture and the packing, not by the controller, so the best it can do
# is meter them: below some tilt nothing moves, above it a lump goes.
uint8 MATERIAL_LIQUID=0
uint8 MATERIAL_GRANULAR=1
uint8 material

# How reluctantly it flows, 0 to 1, WITHIN its class.
#
#   liquid   0 = water or alcohol      1 = honey or syrup     (viscosity)
#   granular 0 = dry salt or sugar     1 = damp clumping flour (cohesion)
#
# It selects nothing by itself. It interpolates every rate, delay and settle
# bound between the two endpoints the controller is configured with, so one
# number moves the whole profile together: a higher index pours slower, waits
# longer for the stream to arrive, expects a larger tail after the tilt stops,
# and holds still longer before believing the scale.
#
# It is a PRIOR, not a measurement. The controller re-estimates the tail from
# what this pour actually did and uses the measured value for the trim pulses
# that follow, so an index that is wrong by a little costs one pulse, not the
# pour.
float64 flow_index

# Wrist angular velocity ceiling [rad/s]. The pour joint is the one whose axis
# tilts the held vessel, so this is also the vessel's tilt rate. It caps the
# profile's own rate; it does not replace it.
float64 max_tilt_rate

# Absolute bound on the tilt this pour may reach [rad], measured from the
# attitude the vessel was carried in. It is what stops a pour that the scale
# never satisfies -- an empty vessel, a blocked spout, a scale that reads zero.
float64 max_tilt

# How close to target_grams counts as done [g].
#
# A floor applies that this cannot cross: the controller cannot resolve below
# one dose quantum, and for a pour that has broken into drops the quantum is set
# by the lip, not by the gains. Ask for less and the pour ends at the floor and
# says so rather than chasing a tolerance the geometry cannot deliver.
float64 tolerance

# Give up after this long [s], counted from the first commanded tilt.
float64 timeout

# Where the held vessel's marker was measured, and the arm configuration it was
# measured in -- for a pour whose grasp was measured EARLIER than the goal, as a
# replay does right after its jaws close on the vessel, with the vessel still on
# the bench 0.4 m from a side camera rather than hung 0.95 m from it. Only read
# under `pour_geometry: measured`.
#
# The controller works out the marker's place relative to its own EE from these
# with its own kinematic model, so the grasp stays one rigid offset measured
# once. Empty grasp_joints (the default) means it measures the marker itself
# when the goal starts, off its configured topic.
#
#   grasp_joints   the arm's joint positions at that moment, in the
#                  controller's joint order [rad]
#   grasp_marker   the marker's centre then, in the arm's base frame [m]
float64[] grasp_joints
geometry_msgs/Point grasp_marker

# Which way the pour joint turns to bring the lip down: +1 or -1, or 0 (the
# default) for the controller's configured pour_direction.
int8 pour_direction

# An arm configuration showing HOW this pour tips the vessel -- a replay passes
# the recording's deepest tilt. The controller tips the vessel about the axis
# its EE turns about from where the goal starts to this configuration, which
# fixes both the axis and which way the lip goes down; pour_direction is then
# not used. Recordings do not agree on this: the first real-cell ones poured by
# rolling the wrist about the approach, later ones by turning about the jaws'
# closing axis, and a config file cannot know which the next one does.
# Empty (the default): tip about the pour joint's axis, pour_direction's way.
float64[] pour_reference_joints
---
bool is_completed

# What the scale read, minus container_grams, once the vessel was upright and
# the reading had settled. This is the number the pour delivered; it is NOT the
# reading at the moment the tilt stopped, which is always short by whatever was
# still in the air and long by the stream's impact on the pan.
float64 final_grams

# How far the vessel had tilted at the furthest point, and how long the goal
# ran. Both are measurements: the executed tilt is the integrated command, which
# is what the vessel actually saw.
float64 peak_tilt
float64 elapsed

# How many trim pulses it took after the bulk phase. 0 means the bulk phase
# landed inside tolerance on its own.
int32 trim_pulses

# What actually came out after the tilt stopped, measured as the difference
# between the reading when the command stopped and the settled reading. This is
# the quantity flow_index only guesses at, and it is worth logging: it is the
# right seed for the next pour of the same material.
float64 measured_afterflow

# Empty on success. On failure this says which bound stopped the pour, so an
# operator can tell "tilted as far as allowed and nothing came out" from "the
# scale went quiet" from "the vessel on the pan was not the one the goal
# described".
string message
---
float64 current_grams
float64 tilt
float64 elapsed

# Estimated outflow [g/s], from the scale, not from the model. Zero whenever the
# controller is not currently reading flow.
float64 flow_rate

# Which phase the pour is in, for an operator watching a goal that legitimately
# spends seconds holding still.
uint8 PHASE_VERIFY=0
uint8 PHASE_SEEK=1
uint8 PHASE_BULK=2
uint8 PHASE_RETRACT=3
uint8 PHASE_SETTLE=4
uint8 PHASE_TRIM=5
uint8 PHASE_DONE=6
uint8 phase
